  "summary": "The Coalition for a Prosperous America has urged US Treasury and US trade office to reject downstream aluminum exemptions for Mexico and Canada in the review of the US-Mexico-Canada Agreement and raise tariffs on fabricated products. The Trump administration's April rule targeting cartel money-laundering has caused unintended economic harm to legitimate small companies along the US-Mexico border, affecting business operations and cash flows. The Mexican civil aviation authority will undergo US FAA assessment this week on safety standards compliance, with potential consequences for Mexico's aviation category ranking.",
  "lead": {
    "h1": "US aluminum industry opposes tariff exemptions for Mexico and Canada in US-Mexico-Canada trade agreement renegotiation",
    "context": "The Coalition for a Prosperous America has urged US Treasury and US trade office to reject downstream aluminum exemptions for Mexico and Canada in the review of the US-Mexico-Canada Agreement and raise tariffs on fabricated products.",
    "background": "Section 232 tariffs on metals sit outside US-Mexico-Canada trade agreement and can theoretically be applied regardless of the trade pact's origin rules, which is why the aluminum lobby wants the two kept separate rather than allowing US-Mexico-Canada trade agreement-qualified Mexican extrusions to bypass the 50% metals duty.",
    "view": "The Coalition's own numbers show why this fight matters to producers: a landed cost gap of US$2.39 versus US$3.88 per pound, about 38%, would price US extrusion makers out of contracts if Mexico wins an exemption. Mexico's ask is not free, either, since exporters have already paid close to US$23 billion in tariffs in the year through April 2026, so an exemption would reverse a cost that has been real and recent, not hypothetical.",
    "prediction": "The more likely outcome is a narrower compromise, such as quotas or partial relief tied to origin verification, rather than a full exemption or a full rejection. That expectation would be wrong if US trade office or Treasury issues a blanket ruling either exempting Mexican and Canadian aluminum entirely or explicitly rejecting any relief.",
    "analysisV": 7,
    "drivers": "",
    "implications": "",
    "next": "",
