      "headline": "US money-transfer rule near Mexico border damages small businesses",
      "context": "The Trump administration's April rule targeting cartel money-laundering has caused unintended economic harm to legitimate small companies along the US-Mexico border, affecting business operations and cash flows.",
      "background": "The rule cut the cash-transaction reporting threshold for financial-services businesses near the border from $10,000 to $200, sweeping in ordinary grocery-store transfer counters used by immigrant families to pay bills and send money home.",
      "view": "The mechanism is a data-collection net built for cartel laundering that instead catches small legal businesses lacking compliance staff to handle reporting at that volume. Remittances brought Mexico about $62.47bn in 2025, more than foreign direct investment, and that flow depends on cheap, accessible transfer points like Nachita's staying open. A rule that raises the cost of running those counters shrinks the informal channels households use to move money, without a public estimate of how much cartel activity it actually stops.",
      "prediction": "Small transfer businesses near the border likely keep absorbing compliance costs or shut down transfer services rather than fight the rule, since litigation is slow and Congress has not acted. That would be wrong if the Treasury narrows the threshold back up or exempts small retailers, which has not been reported.",
      "analysisV": 7,
      "drivers": "",
      "implications": "",
      "next": "",
