      "headline": "Mexico aviation authority faces FAA safety evaluation",
      "context": "The Mexican civil aviation authority will undergo US FAA assessment this week on safety standards compliance, with potential consequences for Mexico's aviation category ranking.",
      "background": "The FAA's IASA program checks whether a country's aviation regulator has the staff, rules and oversight capacity required by the International Civil Aviation Organization. Mexico lost its Category 1 rating in 2021 and has been working since 2023 to regain it.",
      "view": "A one-week visit will not settle the rating either way, since the specialist quoted expects any conclusion to take at least six months. The relevant comparison is Mexico's three-year record since losing Category 1 in 2023, not this week's visit, so the practical test is whether AFAC can show new inspection staffing and updated regulation rather than a single inspection outcome.",
      "prediction": "AFAC most likely receives a preliminary readout with a formal decision deferred for months, not an immediate category change. That view fails if the FAA issues any determination, positive or negative, within the six-month window the specialist named.",
      "analysisV": 7,
